The problem with polls is that they usually allow limited options.
Thus, the true opinions of the people polled are skewed.  For example,
the "confirmation poll" recently taken on this newsgroup had a major
flaw, in that it basically allowed two opinions:

1. I like the game to stay the way it is, and
2. I want "instant death" dangerous actions to be confirmed.

This leaves no room for those who, like myself, prefer the "instant
death" dangerous actions to be changed to "death in three or four
turns depending on various factors."  In this way the game would
become more interesting rather than less so, and although one would
still have to exercise caution, a slip of the fingers would not
instantly terminate one's game.

For example, consider cockatrices.  Suppose that instead of dying in a
few turns while being stoned, one died instantly.  This would be
extremely frustrating.  But adding confirmation to prevent this would
remove the fun of having such a dangerous creature in the game.  The
few turns while one's limbs stiffen give one a chance at life, as well
as adding interest, while not removing one's fear of cockatrices.

Dave Hairston made some excellent suggestions a while back about the
various things that could happen while slowly and painfully drowning
in a pool.  I think it's unfortunate that the discussion has centered
on confirmation/no confirmation, while consideration of Dave's
suggestions would, I believe, satisfy both camps.

In short, I think we'd all prefer a slow, gruesome, and perhaps
avoidable death to either

           "You typed the wrong character!  You die instantly!"
or
           "You typed the wrong character!  Die instantly? (y/n)"

-- Orca Starbuck

